\section{When to Use Adaptive $\bstar$}
\label{app:decision}

Table~\ref{tab:decision} summarizes the boundary conditions identified in \S\ref{sec:robustness} and \S\ref{sec:sensitivity}.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.2cm}ccp{1.5cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Req'd?} & \textbf{Evidence} & \textbf{$\Delta$ PPL if violated} \\
\midrule
Detector AUC $\geq 0.9$ & Yes & N-gram (0.73) degrades & $+$0.129 \\
Detector ECE $\leq 0.05$ & Yes & GPT-2 (0.131) degrades & $+$0.098 \\
Contamination $\rho \geq 0.4$ & Yes & $\rho{=}0.2$ degrades & $+$0.046 \\
Generator $\neq$ trainee (scale gap) & Rec. & 1.7B $\Delta{=}{-}0.013$, OOD inconsistent & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Decision summary for practitioners.
All $\Delta$ values are relative to uniform training (positive = worse).
When any required condition is violated, uniform training is the safer default; the adaptive dual constraint limits degradation relative to unconstrained reweighting but cannot overcome fundamentally inadequate detector signal.}
\label{tab:decision}
\end{table}

